Prisma Browser on Gentoo
=======================
This is an unofficial repackaging of Palo Alto Networks' amd64 Debian binary.
Gentoo is not an upstream-supported platform. An organization account and
applicable license/policy are needed for managed use.

Launch prisma-access-browser as your regular desktop user. For native Wayland,
try --ozone-platform=wayland. Verify chrome://sandbox. Never disable sandboxing.
Portage owns upgrades; Debian/APT repository and cron integration is omitted.
User profiles are preserved on removal.

Protection (optional USE=protection)
-----------------------------------
This installs the upstream Python/eBPF components and a systemd unit, but does
not enable or start it. Policies requiring protection can prevent browser launch
until it is working. Enable with:
    systemctl enable --now pab-protection
Inspect:
    journalctl -u pab-protection

BCC needs build headers for the running kernel, BPF syscall/JIT/events, kprobes,
tracepoints and the kernel functions used by the vendor filters. For the LSM
filter, CONFIG_BPF_LSM=y and bpf in /sys/kernel/security/lsm are needed. Upstream
also implements a kprobe fallback. Loading successfully and satisfying your
organization's policy must be verified on the actual running kernel.

No boot parameters, GRUB files, AppArmor policy, default browser preferences,
or service activation are changed by this ebuild. The service needs host access
to BPF and the browser processes; do not isolate its network namespace because
its browser handshake uses an abstract Unix socket.

Device trust
------------
The upstream optional chrome-management-service setup is not enabled here.
It requires a chromemgmt group, setgid service binary, and a group-writable
/etc/pab/policies/enrollment/DeviceTrustSigningKey. Do not assume device-trust
policies work until enrollment is integrated and tested with your administrator.

Validation status
-----------------
Packaging and library checks do not establish that enterprise sign-in, device
trust, DRM, screen sharing, or protection work. These need desktop and tenant
validation. The protection service is optional to avoid pulling BCC/LLVM into
installations whose policies do not require it; disabling the flag does not
bypass a policy enforced by the browser.
